{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>And he said, 'She is more righteous than I.'</b> Not like the measure of the Holy One, blessed be He is the measure of flesh and blood: a man comes before a judge — if he admits his offense, he kills him; but the Holy One, blessed be He — if one admits and forsakes, He will have compassion. And immediately when Reuben saw that Judah admitted, he arose and admitted regarding the disturbance of his father's bed. And this is what Eliphaz said to Job: ״אשר חכמים יגידו ולא כחדו מאבותם״ — these are Judah and Reuben; and therefore to them alone was the land given, and no stranger passed among them. And this is it: when Moses our teacher, peace be upon him, blessed them, there is no break between them, as it is said: ״יחי ראובן ואל ימות״ etc. ״וזאת ליהודה ויאמר שמע״ — thus no stranger passed among them." "<b>More righteous than I.</b> Its meaning is as its Targum: 'She is pregnant from me.' And one wonders — on the contrary, it is a great act of prostitution to lie with one's daughter-in-law. And one may answer that such was their practice, for when the deceased had no brothers, the next of kin would perform yibum, and this is what it says: 'She is more righteous than I,' for therefore I did not give her to Shelah my son, and it was upon me to perform yibum; I did not see that Shelah should perform yibum. And at the time Judah said, 'She is more righteous than I,' the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: You saved four lives — three from fire and one from the pit; by your life, I will rescue your sons — one from the pit and three from the fire, as it is said: ״ויהיו שם מבני יהודה דניאל חנניה מישאל ועזריה״ — of the sons of Hezekiah it is not said, but rather 'of the sons of Judah.' Daniel from the pit; Hananiah, Mishael, and Azariah from the fire. Another explanation: 'She is more righteous than I' — she is pregnant from me, and I came to her in kiddushin, as we explained above, and not in prostitution. And the Rav Moses objected: but behold, kiddushin do not take effect on one's daughter-in-law; and according to what is concluded in tractate Sotah — 'I am an orphan' — it works out, and Rashi there too explained: 'I am an orphan, and the kiddushin of your son are not valid kiddushin,' for even if she refused him now, she would be found not to have been his daughter-in-law. And according to this, the words of the commentators also work out well: ״ולא יסף עוד לדעתה״ — he did not cease coming to her. And it seems to me that this opinion follows the one who says: even if her son is riding on her shoulders, she may refuse. Another explanation: 'She is more righteous than I' — even if she was pregnant, she was not liable according to the law of a priest's daughter, for the court of Shem decreed only regarding a gentile who comes upon a daughter of Israel, but not regarding an Israelite who comes upon a daughter of Israel. And thus it is stated in Avodah Zarah, chapter אין מעמידין: prostitution — the court of Shem decreed, etc."], :book :genesis, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:06:50.226394471Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}